    text: "Creative hobbies transform everyday life into opportunities for artistic expression, offering both personal fulfilment and a rich vocabulary for communication. Painting encompasses watercolour (translucent, flowing washes), oil painting (rich, layered textures), and acrylic (versatile, fast-drying). Each medium has its techniques: blending, layering, stippling (dotting), and glazing (thin transparent layers over dry paint). Drawing uses pencil, charcoal, ink, and pastels, with techniques like shading, cross-hatching, and perspective. Photography has evolved from darkrooms and film to digital sensors and editing software, but the language persists: aperture, shutter speed, exposure, composition, depth of field, and bokeh (blurred background). Pottery and ceramics involve throwing (shaping clay on a wheel), hand-building (coiling and slab techniques), glazing, and firing in a kiln. Creative writing spans fiction, poetry, non-fiction, and screenwriting, with terms like draft, revision, plot, character arc, dialogue, and narrative voice. Music composition uses melody, harmony, rhythm, chord progression, and arrangement. Woodworking transforms timber through sawing, planing, chiselling, sanding, and finishing with stain or varnish. Each creative hobby develops not just artistic skills but a precise technical vocabulary that enriches learners' English with vivid, descriptive language applicable far beyond the studio or workshop.",
